# n409.aiknol.com — the public edge. This is the only N409 surface on the
# internet; everything behind it binds loopback.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS. The block below is the `n409.aiknol.com` site block as it
# is actually deployed, kept here so the routing is reviewable in the repository
# rather than only readable over ssh. It is NOT a whole Caddyfile and must not
# be installed as one: 204.168.241.124 runs a single native Caddy
# (`/etc/caddy/Caddyfile`, systemd unit `caddy`, v2.11) that also serves two
# unrelated products from the same file. Applying this file wholesale would
# take those down. Edit the corresponding block in place, or `import` this one.
#
# WHY IT IS WRITTEN DOWN AT ALL. What was in this file before described a
# deployment that has never existed on this host: a Caddy running in Docker,
# reached at `host.docker.internal:3000` (there is no Docker on the box at all),
# and a single upstream for every path. Following it would have produced a site
# that could not reach its own backend, and — because the SCIM routes are the
# one public path that does *not* go through the web service — would have
# silently 404'd every SCIM provisioning request from every configured IdP.
# Stale infrastructure config is worse than none: it invites someone to apply it.
#
# THE ONE RULE THAT IS NOT OBVIOUS. `handle` blocks are evaluated in order and
# only the first match runs, so the `/scim/v2/*` block must stay above the
# catch-all. Reordering them routes SCIM to the web service, which has no such
# routes, and the failure is a 404 from an IdP's provisioning job — visible in
# somebody else's logs, not in ours.

n409.aiknol.com {
	# SCIM 2.0 provisioning lives on the valuation service (3001) and is
	# deliberately outside the web service's /api/v1 proxy: it is its own
	# protocol with its own auth (bearer tokens issued by
	# /api/v1/admin/sso/scim-tokens), not part of the application API. So it is
	# forwarded here, directly, rather than through 3000.
	handle /scim/v2/* {
		reverse_proxy localhost:3001
	}

	# Everything else — the SPA and the API alike — goes to the web service,
	# whose own /api/v1 proxy already covers SAML (/api/v1/auth/saml/*) and
	# admin SSO (/api/v1/admin/sso/*). One upstream, so there is no second place
	# for a route to be forgotten.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy localhost:3000
	}
}

# Deliberately absent, each for a reason:
#
#   encode — the web service compresses its own responses, and verified so: the
#   document and its JS assets come back `content-encoding: zstd` and `gzip`
#   respectively over the live site. A second `encode` here would recompress
#   what is already compressed.
#
#   header_up X-Real-IP — nothing reads it. `clientIp.ts` resolves the caller
#   from X-Forwarded-For under Fastify's `trustProxy`, which Caddy populates by
#   default, and adding a second header carrying the same fact is a second thing
#   to keep honest.
#
#   tls — Caddy provisions and renews Let's Encrypt automatically for this
#   hostname, and :80 is redirected to :443 without being asked. An explicit
#   block here would only be a way to get it wrong.
